string.documentation:
Returns the Unicode code point of the character at a position, or `undefined` when the position names no character.

@param position - Optional int position, default `0`. `0` is the first character. Positions count whole Unicode characters, so an accented letter or an emoji is one position. A negative position or one at or past the end gives `undefined`; positions do not count from the end.

@returns The code point as an int from 0 through 1114111. The result type is `int | undefined`; use `??` for a fallback or compare with `undefined` before using it as an int.

```tsrust
const text = "aé😀";
console.log(text.codePointAt(1) ?? -1, text.codePointAt(2) ?? -1, text.codePointAt(3) ?? -1); // prints "233 128512 -1"
```

```tsrust
const code = "A".codePointAt();
if (code !== undefined) { console.log(code + 1, String.fromCodePoint(code + 1)); } // prints "66 B"
```

More than one argument is an `arity` error; a number or string position is a `type` error. JavaScript counts positions in UTF-16 units, so positions after a character above U+FFFF differ there, and a position inside such a character gives half of it. Here every position is a whole character.

The string is only read. The result is a copied int.

Returns the UTF-16 code unit of the character at a position as a number, or `NaN` when the position names no character.

@param position - Optional int position, default `0`. `0` is the first character. Positions count whole Unicode characters. A negative position or one at or past the end gives `NaN`.

@returns A `number` from 0 through 65535, or `NaN`. For a character above U+FFFF the result is its first (high surrogate) unit; use `codePointAt` to get the whole code point.

```tsrust
const text = "a€😀";
console.log(text.charCodeAt(0), text.charCodeAt(1), text.charCodeAt(2), text.charCodeAt(9)); // prints "97 8364 55357 NaN"
```

The result is a `number`, not an int, because `NaN` marks a missing character; test it with `Number.isNaN`. More than one argument is an `arity` error; a number or string position is a `type` error. JavaScript counts positions in UTF-16 units, so positions after a character above U+FFFF differ there.

The string is only read. The result is a copied number.

Array.documentation:
Tests whether a value occurs in the array.

@param value - The value to look for. For `int[]`, `number[]`, `boolean[]` or `string[]` it has the element type. For an array of a primitive union such as `(int | string)[]` it has one of the alternative types; an element whose active alternative is a different type never matches, except that int and number compare by value. A value that is itself a union is a `type` error; narrow it with `typeof` first.
@param fromIndex - Optional int position where the search starts, default `0`. A negative value counts back from the end.

@returns `true` when an element equals the value. `NaN` matches `NaN`, and `0` matches `-0`.

```tsrust
const values: int[] = [1, 2, 3];
console.log(values.includes(2), values.includes(2, 2)); // prints "true false"
```

```tsrust
const mixed: (int | string | null)[] = [1, "two", null];
console.log(mixed.includes("two"), mixed.includes(null), mixed.includes(2)); // prints "true true false"
```

Arrays of records, nested arrays, or unions that contain them are an `unsupported` error, because identity comparison of owned values is not defined; use `some()` or `findIndex()` with a predicate. One or two arguments are required (`arity`).

The array and the value are only read.

Returns the position of the first element that equals a value, or `-1` when none does.

@param value - The value to look for. For `int[]`, `number[]`, `boolean[]` or `string[]` it has the element type. For an array of a primitive union such as `(int | string)[]` it has one of the alternative types; an element whose active alternative is a different type never matches, except that int and number compare by value. A value that is itself a union is a `type` error; narrow it with `typeof` first.
@param fromIndex - Optional int position where the search starts, default `0`. A negative value counts back from the end.

@returns The int position, or `-1`. `NaN` never matches; use `includes()` to test for `NaN`.

```tsrust
const values: int[] = [1, 2, 3, 2];
console.log(values.indexOf(2), values.indexOf(2, 2), values.indexOf(9)); // prints "1 3 -1"
```

```tsrust
const mixed: (int | string)[] = [3, "b", 1, "b"];
console.log(mixed.indexOf("b"), mixed.indexOf(1), mixed.indexOf("z")); // prints "1 2 -1"
```

Arrays of records, nested arrays, or unions that contain them are an `unsupported` error, because identity comparison of owned values is not defined; use `some()` or `findIndex()` with a predicate. One or two arguments are required (`arity`).

The array and the value are only read.

Returns the position of the last element that equals a value, or `-1` when none does. The search runs backward.

@param value - The value to look for. For `int[]`, `number[]`, `boolean[]` or `string[]` it has the element type. For an array of a primitive union such as `(int | string)[]` it has one of the alternative types; an element whose active alternative is a different type never matches, except that int and number compare by value. A value that is itself a union is a `type` error; narrow it with `typeof` first.
@param fromIndex - Optional int position where the backward search starts, default the last element. A negative value counts back from the end.

@returns The int position, or `-1`. `NaN` never matches.

```tsrust
const values: int[] = [1, 2, 3, 2];
console.log(values.lastIndexOf(2), values.lastIndexOf(2, 2), values.lastIndexOf(9)); // prints "3 1 -1"
```

```tsrust
const mixed: (int | string)[] = [3, "b", 1, "b"];
console.log(mixed.lastIndexOf("b"), mixed.lastIndexOf(3)); // prints "3 0"
```

Arrays of records, nested arrays, or unions that contain them are an `unsupported` error, because identity comparison of owned values is not defined; use `some()` or `findIndex()` with a predicate. One or two arguments are required (`arity`).

The array and the value are only read.

Returns a new array containing the elements whose boolean predicate succeeds, preserving their original order.

@param callback - Reusable callback with optional element, index and source arguments; must return boolean.

```tsrust
let values:int[]=[1,2,3];console.log(values.filter((x:int):boolean => { return x > 1; }).length); // prints "2"
```

The method supplies element, integer index and source array in those positions; the callback may declare fewer parameters. Owned element/source arguments are read-only borrows. Receiver is borrowed for observations and copying; independent array results copy owned descendants.

For data-element callbacks, additional trailing optional or defaulted parameters receive undefined. A default initializer runs for each reached callback invocation when its supplied value is undefined; a present null does not trigger it. Empty input and unvisited short-circuit positions do not run callback defaults. Required fixed parameters beyond the supplied positions are rejected. Callable-element mutate visitors remain limited to element and optional index positions; extra parameters or an overlapping source-array parameter are rejected.

```tsrust
const values = [3,1,2];
const filtered = values.filter((value: int, index: int, source: ReadOnly<int[]>, extra: int = 2): boolean => { return value > extra; });
console.log(filtered.length, filtered[0]);
// 1 3
```

Ownership: the receiver and owned callback inputs are borrowed for Read access; the independent result copies retained owned descendants.

A terminal homogeneous rest parameter collects only the remaining arguments actually supplied by this method, in order, into a fresh owning array. After the full documented fixed prefix it receives an empty array. Undefined padding for optional/defaulted fixed parameters does not become a tail element; an actually supplied undefined remains a value and must satisfy the rest element type. The supplied data tuple is element, index and source. Owned element and source-array arguments remain borrowed and cannot transfer into a rest pack. Mutate callable-element visitors retain their restricted element and optional index tuple; a lent environment cannot transfer into the pack.

```tsrust
const values:int[]=[2,3];const result=values.filter((value:int,index:int,source:int[],...tail:int[]):boolean=>{return tail.length===0&&value>index;});console.log(result.join(","));
// 2,3
```

Ownership: the rest pack is independent; Copy tail values are copied and eligible owned tail values transfer without hidden clones. Fixed-prefix borrowing, receiver loans and callback result ownership stay unchanged.

A checked callback error stops callback execution and propagates to the enclosing catch; applicable finally blocks run.

