Replaces elements from start inclusive to end exclusive with value. Defaults are zero and length; negative bounds count from the end and clamp. Mutates the receiver and returns void.

@param value - Same-type element transferred into fill and cloned into independent positions.
@param start - Element start offset. Negative values count from the end; bounds clamp. Defaults to zero.
@param end - Exclusive element bound, default array length. Negative values count from the end and bounds clamp.

```tsrust
let values:int[]=[1,2,3];values.fill(9, 1, 2);console.log(values[1]); // prints "9"
```

Requires mutable access. An owned fill value transfers into the operation and is cloned into independent positions; use clone(value) to preserve the original owner.

Copies the range from start inclusive to end exclusive into the receiver beginning at target, preserving overlapping-copy behavior. end defaults to length; negative bounds count from the end and clamp. Returns void.

@param target - Destination element offset; negative indices count from the end and bounds clamp.
@param start - Element start offset. Negative values count from the end; bounds clamp. Required.
@param end - Exclusive element bound, default array length. Negative values count from the end and bounds clamp.

```tsrust
let values:int[]=[1,2,3];values.copyWithin(0, 1);console.log(values[0]); // prints "2"
```

Requires mutable access. Owned elements are deeply copied through a temporary snapshot, unlike JavaScript shared references.

Returns a copy with deleteCount elements removed at start and optional items inserted. Negative start counts from the end; bounds clamp. Omitted deleteCount removes the suffix.

@param start - Element start offset. Negative values count from the end; bounds clamp. Required.
@param deleteCount - Number of elements to delete, clamped to the available suffix; omission deletes that suffix.
@param items - Zero or more same-type elements to insert at start.

```tsrust
let values:int[]=[1,2,3];console.log(values.toSpliced(1, 1, 9)[1]); // prints "9"
```

Receiver is borrowed for observations and copying; independent array results copy owned descendants.

Returns the element at a signed index without changing the array or its length. Takes exactly one int argument with no default, in the signed 64-bit range -9223372036854775808..9223372036854775807. Omission, extra arguments, Number or other argument types are errors. Unlike JavaScript numeric indexing, there is no numeric coercion or fractional-index truncation. Zero selects the first element; a negative index counts backward from the current end, so -1 selects the last. An out-of-range index, including an extreme signed int, returns undefined rather than a bounds error. The return type is the element type or undefined, with duplicate undefined alternatives removed.

```tsrust
const values:int[]=[10,20,30];console.log(String(values.at(0)),String(values.at(-1)),values.length); // 10 30 3
const empty:int[]=[];console.log(String(empty.at(0)),String(empty.at(-1)));const single:int[]=[7];console.log(String(single.at(1)),String(single.at(-2)));
// undefined undefined
// undefined undefined
```

Supports existing dense homogeneous scalar, nested-array and record families, including declared union elements. A stored null remains null; an out-of-range selection returns undefined. Sparse arrays, untyped heterogeneous arrays and implicit index coercion are unsupported. Number elements preserve their original IEEE bits. An undefined element and an out-of-range result are both undefined. Nullable array receivers and optional results need a strict null/undefined guard before payload member access. The receiver and index evaluate once in that order.

Borrows a reusable receiver; copyable elements return copied optional values. Named owned receivers yield scoped read-only optional views, usable by Read nullable consumers. While a view is live, conflicting receiver mutation or moves are prohibited; the view cannot mutate, consume, return or escape its payload. Explicit clone(view) owns an independent optional value. Temporary owned receivers can transfer the selected payload without an implicit clone.

Returns the first element whose predicate returns true, or undefined when none matches. Visits indices from zero upward; stops immediately at the first successful visit in that direction. The callback receives original indices and the full receiver, which is not changed. Takes exactly one reusable callback, with no default. Its result must be boolean, without truthiness coercion. It supplies element, index:int, and a read-only source array in those positions; the callback may declare fewer parameters. Missing or extra method arguments, an incompatible parameter type, a non-boolean result, or once-only/consuming callbacks are errors.

@param callback - Reusable strict-boolean predicate; optional element, original integer index and read-only source parameters.

```tsrust
const values:int[]=[1,2,3];const selected=values.find((value:int,index:int,source:int[]):boolean=>{return value>1&&source[index]===value;});console.log(String(selected),values.join("|")); // 2 1|2|3
let calls:int=0;const empty:int[]=[];console.log(String(empty.find(():boolean=>{calls+=1;return true;})),calls);const missing:int[]=[1,2,3];console.log(String(missing.find((value:int):boolean=>{calls+=1;return value>9;})),calls);
// undefined 0
// undefined 3
```

Receiver and predicate expressions evaluate once in that order, even for an empty array; an empty array invokes no callbacks. Supports existing dense homogeneous scalar, nested-array and record families. Sparse arrays and prototype overrides are unsupported; mixed or nullable elements need a declared union element type such as `(int | string)[]`. Number results preserve their original IEEE bits. Undefined elements and no match both produce undefined; canonical duplicate alternatives collapse. Nullable receivers and optional payloads need strict null/undefined guards before member access. The predicate may change unrelated captured state, but cannot mutate or consume the element or receiver.

