Prepends zero or more same-type elements in argument order and returns the new int length. Existing elements follow the inserted elements in their original order. With no arguments, returns the current length without changing contents. The receiver evaluates once, then every argument evaluates once from left to right before the outer insertion commits. If argument evaluation changes the receiver through a permitted operation, insertion uses its resulting current contents and length.

@param values - Zero or more elements of exactly the receiver element type; arguments preserve their order at the front. Arrays are elements only when the receiver itself has an array element type; they are not flattened.

```tsrust
const values:int[]=[3,4];console.log(values.unshift(1,2),values.join("|")); // 4 1|2|3|4
const empty:int[]=[];console.log(empty.unshift(),empty.length);const single:int[]=[7];console.log(single.unshift(),single.join("|"));
// 0 0
// 1 7
```

Supports existing dense homogeneous scalar, nested-array and record families. No coercion or sparse arrays are accepted; mixed or nullable elements need a declared union element type such as `(int | string)[]`. Nullable receivers require a proven payload guard. The result is a signed native int length. The post-argument resulting length must fit int and ordinary Rust capacity limits before the outer mutation; allocation can fail. Zero arguments need only read access and preserve fixed/read-only views. Nonzero insertion requires mutable receiver access and shifts existing elements toward higher indices.

Transfers owned arguments without cloning; inserted owners become receiver elements. Borrowed indexed owners need an explicit clone before insertion, and incoming loans prohibit conflicting mutation or moves. The receiver remains reusable; zero-argument observation does not consume it.

Removes and returns a range of elements, inserting zero or more same-type items at that position. Existing and inserted elements retain their order. The result is an independent, mutable array of removed elements. With no arguments, returns an empty array without changing the receiver. With only start, removes the remaining tail. An explicit deleteCount of zero removes nothing, so omitted deletion and zero deletion differ.

@param start - Optional signed int offset; a negative value counts from the current end. Clamp into 0..length. Omitted start means the zero-argument no-change operation.
@param deleteCount - Optional signed int count; with start alone, remove the remaining tail. Zero or negative counts remove nothing; a positive count is capped at the remaining length. Explicit undefined is not accepted.
@param items - Zero or more elements exactly matching the receiver element type, inserted in argument order without flattening.

```tsrust
const values:int[]=[1,2,3,4];const removed=values.splice(1,2,8,9);console.log(removed.join("|"),values.join("|")); // 2|3 1|8|9|4
```

Receiver and every argument evaluate once from left to right before the outer splice commits. Normalization uses the post-argument current length, including permitted receiver changes made by argument evaluation. Start and deleteCount accept the full signed 64-bit int range; start clamps safely and deletion is capped at the remaining length. Number arguments, coercion, sparse arrays and prototype hooks are unsupported; mixed or nullable elements need a declared union element type such as `(int | string)[]`. Supports dense homogeneous scalar, nested-array, record and annotated owning-callable families; nullable receivers require a checked guard. The resulting receiver length and removed-output length must both fit native int and ordinary Rust capacity limits, checked before the outer mutation; allocation can fail. Zero arguments need only Read access and preserve fixed/read-only views. Any invocation with arguments requires mutable receiver access. Evaluation failures do not commit the outer splice; completed argument effects remain.

Removed owners move into the returned array and inserted owners transfer into the receiver without implicit cloning. Borrowed indexed data owners require explicit clone. Callable environments cannot be cloned: remove an indexed environment first to transfer it. Compatible owned callbacks can be inserted, and removed callbacks retain their captured state. A removed once callback must be taken out of the result array with remove() before invocation. Incoming/parent loans forbid conflicting mutation or moves. The receiver remains reusable, and the removed array can be changed independently.

Stably orders the receiver in place and returns void. Takes zero or one comparator argument. Ordering is stable: elements comparing equal retain their relative order.

@param compare - Optional reusable callback supplied left and right element positions; it may declare fewer parameters. Copyable parameters are copied; owned parameters are read-only. Each callback must return one concrete numeric type, int or number; tagged mixed return unions are rejected. Its result sign determines ordering: negative means before, positive means after, zero or Number NaN means equal. No Boolean truthiness or coercion, once-only callbacks, consuming/mutating elements, or receiver access during comparison is accepted. Nested-array, record and callable element types require a comparator, even when empty; primitive element types may omit it.

```tsrust
const values:int[]=[10,2,-1,2];values.sort();console.log(values.join("|"));
// -1|2|2|10
```

Without a comparator, int sorts numerically; Number uses native f64 total ordering, distinguishing -0 from +0 and ordering NaNs by their sign and payload bits; false precedes true; String uses UTF-8 lexical order; null and undefined compare equal within their homogeneous families. These native defaults differ from JavaScript string-coercing default sort. UTF-8 ordering places ﬁ before 😀, whereas UTF-16 ordering places that pair in the opposite order. Supports dense arrays of every element type and rank, including declared union elements; sparse arrays, prototype overrides and explicit undefined comparators are unsupported. Nullable receivers require a checked guard. Receiver and comparator expressions evaluate once in that order, including empty input. The comparator may change unrelated captured state; invocation order and count depend on standard Rust sorting. Use a consistent ordering; no key-string formatting is performed. If comparison panics, the receiver may be partially reordered, but every element owner remains preserved.

Requires mutable receiver access. Existing element owners are rearranged without cloning and remain owned by the receiver.

Additional trailing optional or defaulted parameters receive undefined. A default initializer runs for each reached callback invocation when its supplied value is undefined; a present null does not trigger it. Empty input and unvisited short-circuit positions do not run callback defaults. Required fixed parameters beyond the supplied positions are rejected. Existing comparator element access and source-loan restrictions also apply to default initializers.

```tsrust
const values = [3,1,2];
values.sort((left?: int, right?: int, extra: int = 0): int => { return (left ?? 0) - (right ?? 0) + extra; });
console.log(values[0], values[1], values[2]);
// 1 2 3
```

Ownership: comparison requires exclusive receiver access and rearranges existing owners without cloning.

A terminal homogeneous rest parameter collects only the remaining arguments actually supplied by this method, in order, into a fresh owning array. After the full documented fixed prefix it receives an empty array. Undefined padding for optional/defaulted fixed parameters does not become a tail element; an actually supplied undefined remains a value and must satisfy the rest element type. The supplied tuple is left and right. A Copy scalar right value can be collected after a fixed left parameter; owned comparison operands remain read-only borrows and cannot transfer into the pack.

```tsrust
const values:int[]=[3,1,2];values.sort((left:int,...tail:int[]):int=>{return left-tail[0];});console.log(values.join(","));
// 1,2,3
```

Ownership: the rest pack is independent; Copy tail values are copied and eligible owned tail values transfer without hidden clones. Fixed-prefix borrowing, receiver loans and callback result ownership stay unchanged.

A checked callback error stops callback execution and propagates to the enclosing catch; applicable finally blocks run. A comparator error leaves the receiver in its original order.

