Array.documentation:
Returns a stably ordered, independently mutable copy without changing the receiver. Takes zero or one comparator argument. Ordering is stable: elements comparing equal retain their relative order.

@param compare - Optional reusable callback supplied left and right element positions; it may declare fewer parameters. Copyable parameters are copied; owned parameters are read-only. Each callback must return one concrete numeric type, int or number; tagged mixed return unions are rejected. Its result sign determines ordering: negative means before, positive means after, zero or Number NaN means equal. No Boolean truthiness or coercion, once-only callbacks, or consuming/mutating elements are accepted. The comparator may read the original source, but cannot mutate or consume it. Nested-array and record element types require a comparator, even when empty; primitive element types may omit it.

```tsrust
const values:string[]=[clone("é"),clone("a")];const sorted=values.toSorted();sorted[0]=clone("changed");console.log(values.join("|"),sorted.join("|"));
// é|a changed|é
```

Without a comparator, int sorts numerically; Number uses native f64 total ordering, distinguishing -0 from +0 and ordering NaNs by their sign and payload bits; false precedes true; String uses UTF-8 lexical order; null and undefined compare equal within their homogeneous families. These native defaults differ from JavaScript string-coercing default sort. Supports dense arrays of every element type and rank, including declared union elements; sparse arrays, prototype overrides and explicit undefined comparators are unsupported. Nullable receivers require a checked guard. Receiver and comparator expressions evaluate once in that order, including empty input. The comparator may change unrelated captured state; invocation order and count depend on standard Rust sorting. Use a consistent ordering; no key-string formatting is performed. If comparison panics, the copied result is dropped and the source stays unchanged.

Borrows the source for Read access and clones owned descendants into the independent result; source and result remain separately reusable.

Additional trailing optional or defaulted parameters receive undefined. A default initializer runs for each reached callback invocation when its supplied value is undefined; a present null does not trigger it. Empty input and unvisited short-circuit positions do not run callback defaults. Required fixed parameters beyond the supplied positions are rejected. Existing comparator element access and source-loan restrictions also apply to default initializers.

```tsrust
const values = [3,1,2];
const sorted = values.toSorted((left: int, right: int, extra: int = 0): int => { return left - right + extra; });
console.log(sorted[0], sorted[1], sorted[2], values[0]);
// 1 2 3 3
```

Ownership: the source stays borrowed for Read access and owned descendants are independently copied before comparisons.

A terminal homogeneous rest parameter collects only the remaining arguments actually supplied by this method, in order, into a fresh owning array. After the full documented fixed prefix it receives an empty array. Undefined padding for optional/defaulted fixed parameters does not become a tail element; an actually supplied undefined remains a value and must satisfy the rest element type. The supplied tuple is left and right. A Copy scalar right value can be collected after a fixed left parameter; owned comparison operands remain read-only borrows and cannot transfer into the pack.

```tsrust
const values:int[]=[3,1,2];const result=values.toSorted((left:int,...tail:int[]):int=>{return left-tail[0];});console.log(values.join(","),result.join(","));
// 3,1,2 1,2,3
```

Ownership: the rest pack is independent; Copy tail values are copied and eligible owned tail values transfer without hidden clones. Fixed-prefix borrowing, receiver loans and callback result ownership stay unchanged.

A checked callback error stops callback execution and propagates to the enclosing catch; applicable finally blocks run. A comparator error leaves the receiver in its original order.

