Array.documentation:
Returns an independently mutable array with nested array levels removed in encounter order, without changing the receiver. Takes zero or one argument.

@param depth - Optional strict signed int depth, default 1. Values at most 0 make an independent copy without flattening; positive values remove that many nested array levels, capped at the leaf rank. For a nested receiver, depth must be a signed integer literal so the return rank is fixed at compile time. A rank-one receiver accepts a dynamic int and always keeps its rank. The range is -9223372036854775808 through 9223372036854775807. More than one argument, Number values and explicit undefined are rejected.

Supports existing dense homogeneous primitive, String, nested-array and nominal-record families. Empty nested arrays contribute no elements. Homogeneous null[] and undefined[] leaves are supported. Sparse slots, mixed element families, nullable union elements, depth coercion and Infinity overload are unsupported. Result leaf type is unchanged; its concrete rank follows the checked depth. Receiver and any depth expression evaluate once in order, including empty input.

```tsrust
const values:int[][]=[[1,2],[],[3]];const flattened=values.flat();console.log(flattened.join("|"),values.length);
// prints 1|2|3 3
```

Borrows a named source and copies its owned descendants into the independent result. A fresh owned temporary may transfer its elements; no source mutation occurs.

Maps elements from first to last and collects an independently mutable array, without changing the source. Requires exactly one callback.

@param callback - Required reusable callback supplied element value, original int index and read-only complete source; it may declare fewer parameters. Copy elements are copied into callback parameters; owned elements are borrowed for Read access. It returns one fixed concrete scalar, record or array type. A scalar or record result becomes one output element; an array result contributes its elements in order, flattening exactly one level. Mixed scalar/array or nullable result types, once-only callbacks and consuming/mutating element or source parameters are unsupported. Returning a borrowed owned parameter requires explicit clone or a fresh value under ordinary return rules.

Supports existing dense homogeneous families and ranks. Empty callback arrays contribute no elements; nested callback arrays are flattened only once. Homogeneous null[] and undefined[] leaves are supported; nullable union elements, mixed element families, coercion and prototype overrides are unsupported. Receiver and callback expressions evaluate once in that order. The callback runs once per original element with its original index; empty input invokes it zero times. The callback may change unrelated captured state, but cannot mutate or consume the source. The conceptual Result and Flattened labels stand for the one statically checked callback result and concrete output array type.

```tsrust
const values:int[]=[2,3];const mapped=values.flatMap((value:int,index:int,source:int[]):int=>{return value+index+source.length;});console.log(mapped.join("|"),values.join("|"));
// prints 4|6 2|3
```

Borrows a named source for Read access. Callback-owned results transfer their elements into independent output without implicit cloning; borrowed owned parameters require explicit clone to return an independent owner. The source remains borrowed through traversal; callbacks cannot consume its elements.

Stored reusable callable elements can be lent to an arrow visitor through a mutate callable parameter and optional int index. The visitor may produce independent scalar or array results; its returned owners transfer into the flattened output. Captured state stays in each receiver environment. Once elements, escaping the borrowed environment and overlapping source-array parameters are rejected. Arrow visitors may be supplied directly, through a local binding or from a factory; forwarded function declarations and abstract visitor parameters are not yet supported.

Ownership: callable element invocation requires exclusive receiver access. Overlapping receiver capture, mutation and moves are rejected during traversal; receiver environments are not cloned or consumed.

For data-element callbacks, additional trailing optional or defaulted parameters receive undefined. A default initializer runs for each reached callback invocation when its supplied value is undefined; a present null does not trigger it. Empty input and unvisited short-circuit positions do not run callback defaults. Required fixed parameters beyond the supplied positions are rejected. Callable-element mutate visitors remain limited to element and optional index positions; extra parameters or an overlapping source-array parameter are rejected.

```tsrust
const values = [3,1,2];
const flat = values.flatMap((value: int, index: int, source: ReadOnly<int[]>, extra: int = 5): int[] => { return [value + extra]; });
console.log(flat[0], flat[1], flat[2]);
// 8 6 7
```

Ownership: callback-owned results move into the output; source elements stay borrowed throughout defaults and callback execution.

A terminal homogeneous rest parameter collects only the remaining arguments actually supplied by this method, in order, into a fresh owning array. After the full documented fixed prefix it receives an empty array. Undefined padding for optional/defaulted fixed parameters does not become a tail element; an actually supplied undefined remains a value and must satisfy the rest element type. The supplied data tuple is element, index and source. Owned element and source-array arguments remain borrowed and cannot transfer into a rest pack. Mutate callable-element visitors retain their restricted element and optional index tuple; a lent environment cannot transfer into the pack.

```tsrust
const values:int[]=[2,3];const result=values.flatMap((value:int,index:int,source:int[],...tail:int[]):int[]=>{return [value+tail.length];});console.log(result.join(","));
// 2,3
```

Ownership: the rest pack is independent; Copy tail values are copied and eligible owned tail values transfer without hidden clones. Fixed-prefix borrowing, receiver loans and callback result ownership stay unchanged.

A checked callback error stops callback execution and propagates to the enclosing catch; applicable finally blocks run.

