Array.documentation:
Returns an eagerly materialized array of index/value pairs, in receiver order. Takes no arguments; passing an argument is an arity error. Each pair is a fixed tuple: position 0 is its zero-based int index and position 1 has the receiver element type. Empty input produces an empty result. The receiver is unchanged. Supported homogeneous data arrays include primitive, record, nested-array and tuple elements; values containing callable owners cannot be copied.

```tsrust
const words=["cat","dog"];const pairs=words.entries();console.log(pairs[0][0],pairs[0][1],pairs[1][0]);
// 0 cat 1
pairs[0][1]="changed";console.log(words[0],pairs[0][1]);
// cat changed
```

```tsrust
let sum=0;for(const pair of [4,5].entries()){sum+=pair[0]+pair[1];}const empty:int[]=[];console.log(sum,empty.entries().length);
// 10 0
```

The result is an ordinary owning array and works with for...of, Array.from and array methods. It captures values when called; later receiver changes do not update it. There is no iterator object with next() or iterator-symbol protocol. Pair indices are accessed with constant integer positions.

Ownership: the receiver is borrowed once. Owned element descendants are copied into independent pairs; changing a pair never changes the source. The caller owns the result.

console.documentation:
Prints a box-drawn table to stdout. Returns void without changing data. Arrays use indices as row labels; object fields become row labels. Object or array rows expose their own fields or indices as columns; primitive rows use a Values column. A primitive data argument prints normally; omitted data prints undefined.

@param data - Optional primitive (including null/undefined), array or record. Inline object literals and empty arrays are accepted in console arguments.
@param properties - Optional string[] (or undefined for omission) selecting columns in the supplied order. Unknown names produce empty columns; an empty list shows indices only. Omission discovers columns from the rows.

```tsrust
console.table({name:"Pat",age:7});
// Columns: (index), Values; rows: name -> 'Pat', age -> 7.
```

Cells use quoted strings and shallow inspection. Inputs remain unchanged and available after printing.

Nullable values print their present payload, `null`, or `undefined`. For example, `const users = ["Alice"]; console.log(users.pop()); console.log(users.pop());` prints `Alice` then `undefined`. Inspection borrows owned payloads; logging does not consume them.

Inspects one value on stdout rather than interpreting it as a format string. Omitted value prints undefined. Options are a record, null or undefined; omitted fields use defaults. depth defaults to 2 (also when undefined); null requests maximum supported depth; -1 hides aggregate contents, and native inspection caps depth at 128. showHidden defaults to false and adds array length. colors defaults to false and enables ANSI value colors. Other options are ignored.

@param value - Optional printable value.
@param options - Optional record containing depth (int or number), showHidden (boolean) and colors (boolean); null/undefined boolean options use false.

```tsrust
console.dir({a:{b:1}},{depth:0}); // stdout: { a: [Object] }
```

Returns void. Arguments evaluate once left to right; inputs are borrowed and remain unchanged. The supported values are native primitives, arrays and records. Functions, heterogeneous arrays and custom object inspectors are unsupported.

Nullable values print their present payload, `null`, or `undefined`. For example, `const users = ["Alice"]; console.log(users.pop()); console.log(users.pop());` prints `Alice` then `undefined`. Inspection borrows owned payloads; logging does not consume them.

Prints an optional formatted label, then indents subsequent stdout and stderr console lines by two spaces. Collapsed groups display the same way as normal groups. Nested calls add indentation; groupEnd removes one level.

@param values - Zero or more values forming a log-style label; omission prints no label.

```tsrust
console.group("work"); console.log(1); console.groupEnd();
// stdout: work, then two spaces followed by 1.
```

Returns void. Arguments evaluate once left to right; inputs are borrowed and remain unchanged. The supported values are native primitives, arrays and records. Functions, heterogeneous arrays and custom object inspectors are unsupported.

Prints an optional formatted label, then indents subsequent stdout and stderr console lines by two spaces. Collapsed groups display the same way as normal groups. Nested calls add indentation; groupEnd removes one level.

@param values - Zero or more values forming a log-style label; omission prints no label.

```tsrust
console.groupCollapsed("work"); console.log(1); console.groupEnd();
// stdout: work, then two spaces followed by 1.
```

Returns void. Arguments evaluate once left to right; inputs are borrowed and remain unchanged. The supported values are native primitives, arrays and records. Functions, heterogeneous arrays and custom object inspectors are unsupported.

Starts a monotonic timer identified by label. Writes nothing when successful; a duplicate label warns and keeps the existing timer.

@param label - Optional printable label, default "default" when omitted or undefined.

```tsrust
console.time("work");console.timeLog("work",7);console.timeEnd("work");
// stdout: work: <elapsed>ms 7, then work: <elapsed>ms.
```

Use timers to measure how long a section of code takes. Durations are printed in milliseconds with up to three fractional digits. Missing labels write a warning immediately to stderr.

Returns void. Arguments evaluate once left to right; inputs are borrowed and remain unchanged. The supported values are native primitives, arrays and records. Functions, heterogeneous arrays and custom object inspectors are unsupported.

Prints the elapsed duration without removing the timer, followed by optional additional inspected values. Message values do not perform format substitution.

@param label - Optional printable label, default "default" when omitted or undefined.
@param values - Optional extra printable values.

```tsrust
console.time("work");console.timeLog("work",7);console.timeEnd("work");
// stdout: work: <elapsed>ms 7, then work: <elapsed>ms.
```

Use timers to measure how long a section of code takes. Durations are printed in milliseconds with up to three fractional digits. Missing labels write a warning immediately to stderr.

Returns void. Arguments evaluate once left to right; inputs are borrowed and remain unchanged. The supported values are native primitives, arrays and records. Functions, heterogeneous arrays and custom object inspectors are unsupported.

Prints the elapsed duration and removes the timer.

@param label - Optional printable label, default "default" when omitted or undefined.

```tsrust
console.time("work");console.timeLog("work",7);console.timeEnd("work");
// stdout: work: <elapsed>ms 7, then work: <elapsed>ms.
```

Use timers to measure how long a section of code takes. Durations are printed in milliseconds with up to three fractional digits. Missing labels write a warning immediately to stderr.

Returns void. Arguments evaluate once left to right; inputs are borrowed and remain unchanged. The supported values are native primitives, arrays and records. Functions, heterogeneous arrays and custom object inspectors are unsupported.

globals.documentation:
Stores exactly one success payload of type T or error payload of type E. Use it for a fallible operation whose caller must handle failure explicitly. Construct with Ok or Err and inspect with an exhaustive match. Ok(value) and Err(error) arms expose only their active payload. Missing variants and unreachable arms are compile errors; a final _ arm handles the remainder. Owned Function<...> and Once<Function<...>> payloads retain their invocation contract and captured state. Borrowed ReadOnly or Mutable callbacks cannot be stored directly as Result payloads. Matching a ReadOnly<Result<T, E>> parameter lends its selected payload for that arm. The caller keeps the Result; owned payloads cannot escape or be mutated through that borrow. Use clone() explicitly when an independent cloneable payload is needed. Matching a Mutable<Result<T, E>> parameter lends exclusive access to its selected owned payload. Record fields and arrays can change in place; neither the payload nor a closure owning it may escape. Access the payload rather than the Result while that exclusive borrow is active. Scalar payloads copy into their pattern bindings. There are no public value/error fields. A void variant has no payload; construct it without an argument and match it with Ok(_) or Err(_).

```tsrust
const result: Result<int, string> = Ok(5); match (result) { Ok(value) => { console.log(value); }, Err(_) => { console.log(-1); } }
// 5
```

```tsrust
const callback: Result<Function<int>, string> = Ok((): int => 7); match (callback) { Ok(action) => { console.log(action()); }, Err(_) => { console.log(-1); } }
// 7
```

```tsrust
function resultLength(result: ReadOnly<Result<string, int>>): int { match (result) { Ok(text) => { return text.length; }, Err(code) => { return code; } } } const borrowed: Result<string, int> = Ok("abc"); console.log(resultLength(borrowed), resultLength(borrowed));
// 3 3
```

```tsrust
function append(result: Mutable<Result<int[], string>>): void { match (result) { Ok(values) => { values.push(9); }, Err(_) => {} } } const editable: Result<int[], string> = Ok([1]); append(editable); append(editable); match (editable) { Ok(values) => { console.log(values.length); }, Err(_) => {} }
// 3
```

Ownership: Owned payloads transfer into the Result without an implicit clone. Matching an owned Result transfers it and exposes the selected payload in its arm; that binding does not escape its lexical scope unless explicitly returned or transferred.

Constructs a successful Result containing value. A Result<T, E> annotation, call/return context, or explicit type arguments must determine both payload types. For example, use it to return a computed value from a fallible function. Takes exactly one value of type T; when T is void, takes no argument. A wrong payload type or argument count is a compile error. No error payload is created, and no exception is thrown. Use Ok<T, E>(...) when no surrounding Result type supplies that information.

```tsrust
const result = Ok<int, string>(5); match (result) { Ok(value) => { console.log(value); }, Err(_) => { console.log(-1); } }
// 5
```

Ownership: Owned payloads transfer into the Result without an implicit clone. Matching an owned Result transfers it and exposes the selected payload in its arm; that binding does not escape its lexical scope unless explicitly returned or transferred.

Constructs a failed Result containing error. A Result<T, E> annotation, call/return context, or explicit type arguments must determine both payload types. Use it to return an error that the caller must inspect or forward. Takes exactly one error of type E; when E is void, takes no argument. A wrong payload type or argument count is a compile error. No success payload is created, and construction does not throw. Use Err<T, E>(...) when no surrounding Result type supplies that information.

```tsrust
const result = Err<int, string>("failed"); match (result) { Ok(_) => { console.log("unexpected"); }, Err(error) => { console.log(error); } }
// failed
```

Ownership: Owned payloads transfer into the Result without an implicit clone. Matching an owned Result transfers it and exposes the selected payload in its arm; that binding does not escape its lexical scope unless explicitly returned or transferred.

examples.source:
let calls = 0;
const count: Mutable<Function<int>> = (): int => { calls += 1; return calls; };
console.log(count(), count());

const values = [1];
const grow: ReadOnly<Function<int, [Mutable<int[]>]>> = (list: int[]): int => { list.push(2); return list.length; };
console.log(grow(values), values.join(","));

const text = "abc";
const size: ReadOnly<Function<int>> = (): int => { return text.length; };
console.log(size(), size(), text);

const measure: ReadOnly<Function<int, [ReadOnly<string>]>> = (text: string): int => { return text.length; };
const word = "four";
console.log(measure(word), word);

const label = "done".repeat(1);
const finish: Once<Function<string>> = (): string => { return label; };
console.log(finish());

ArrayConstructor.documentation:
Creates a dense, homogeneous array containing its arguments in their original order. Unlike a length constructor, Array.of(3) creates one element whose value is 3. Takes zero or more items and leaves the result independently mutable.

@param items - Variadic elements with one exact supported element type T. T may be inferred from nonempty arguments, supplied by an expected T[] result, or given explicitly as Array.of<T>(...). A typed or contextual empty call is accepted; an empty call without either type source is rejected. Explicit calls take exactly one type argument. Contextual Number elements accept integer literals, but typed int and Number values are not implicitly converted. Arguments evaluate once from left to right; completed argument effects remain if a later argument fails.

```tsrust
const values=Array.of(3,1,2);console.log(values.join("|"));
// prints 3|1|2
```

Supports dense scalar, string, nested-array, record, tuple, union and owning stored-callable elements. Contextual union elements retain their declared alternatives. Homogeneous null[] and undefined[] results are supported, for example `Array.of(null, null)`. Sparse slots and JavaScript coercion are unsupported. The result array rank is at most 128, so a nested-array T has rank at most 127; generic record arguments obey the same type limits. Native lengths and ordinary Rust capacity limits apply; allocation can fail. This is a typed constructor, not a callable general Array constructor or a prototype/subclass hook.

Owned named String, array and record arguments transfer into the new array; they are not implicitly cloned. Borrowed indexed owners and scoped read-only views need an explicit clone before transfer. Copy elements are copied; the returned array owns its elements.

Spread arguments accept arrays, strings, Sets and Maps, for example `Array.of(0, ...new Set<int>([2, 1]), 3)`. Each source evaluates once and its elements are staged before the next argument evaluates. Strings yield Unicode scalar character strings; a combining mark is a separate element. Sets yield insertion-ordered values; Maps yield insertion-ordered key/value tuples. Scalar elements copy. Owned collection elements and Map tuples transfer, including noncloneable values; readonly owners require explicit copying before transfer. String spread constructs character values and leaves its source usable. Empty Set/Map constructors can receive their element types from the expected array result. Array literals use the same rules: `[0, ...values, 3]`. Arbitrary iterator objects remain unsupported.

Creates a new array from an array, the characters of a string, Set values or Map key/value tuples. With a mapping function, each value is transformed before it is stored.

@param source - An array of any supported element type, a string, a typed Set or a typed Map. Arrays retain element order; Sets and Maps retain insertion order. Map items have type [Key, Value]. Strings yield one element per Unicode scalar value, so an emoji is one element. Empty sources produce empty arrays.
@param map - Optional reusable function called once per item, in order, with the item and its zero-based int index. For a Set the item is its value; for a Map it is a readonly [Key, Value] tuple. Exactly two positions are supplied, with no source collection argument. Normal omitted/default/rest rules apply; a third required position is an arity error. A checked callback error stops traversal and reaches surrounding catch/finally.

Returns the source element array when map is omitted: T[] for arrays/Sets, string[] for strings and [Key, Value][] for Maps. With map it returns U[], where U is the mapping result type and T denotes the encountered item type. The source evaluates once and is unchanged. Native copying recursively copies owned descendants into independent results; it does not preserve JavaScript shallow object aliases. Mapping reads borrowed inputs and transfers the mapper's result into the new array, without cloning Map values first.

```tsrust
const source=[3,1,2];const copy=Array.from(source);copy.push(9);console.log(source.join(","),copy.join(","));
// 3,1,2 3,1,2,9
```

```tsrust
console.log(Array.from(new Set<int>([3,1,3]),(value,index)=>{return value*10+index;}).join(","));
// 30,11
```

```tsrust
const values=new Map<string,int>([["a",2],["b",3]]);const pairs=Array.from(values);pairs[0][1]=9;console.log(Array.from(values,([key,value],index)=>{return key+String(value+index);}).join("|"),pairs[0][1]);
// a2|b4 9
```

```tsrust
console.log(Array.from("aé😀").join("|"),Array.from("").length);
// a|é|😀 0
```

No arguments or more than two, including thisArg, are arity errors. Wrong source/item/result types, consuming mappers and receiver mutation during mapping are static errors. Unmapped copies require cloneable owned descendants; mapped Map values need not be cloneable. Spread arguments, arbitrary iterator objects and array-like records with length are unsupported. Native string lengths still count UTF-8 bytes, although iteration visits Unicode scalar values.

Ownership: source and owned mapper inputs borrow readonly for the call; scalar inputs copy. Unmapped owned items are explicitly copied. Mapped outputs belong to the result; retaining a borrowed item requires clone(item). The caller owns the new array.

std:path.documentation:
Returns the last component of a path without reading the filesystem or normalizing dot components. Accepts a string path and an optional string suffix. Trailing separators are ignored for ordinary extraction; suffix removal is case-sensitive. Returns independently owned text and leaves both inputs unchanged.

```tsrust
import { basename } from "std:path";
function main() {
    console.log(basename("/tmp/report.txt")); // report.txt
    console.log(basename("/tmp/report.txt", ".txt")); // report
}
```

An empty path or separator-only root normally returns an empty string. Dot and dot-dot names are preserved. Removing a suffix equal to the entire path returns an empty string; a suffix equal only to a component in a longer path leaves that component intact. Partial suffix matches with trailing separators can preserve the trailing separator, for example basename("/foo/a/", "ba") returns "a/". POSIX paths use /; Windows paths recognize both / and backslash and drive-letter prefixes. Non-string arguments and more than two arguments are compile-time errors. Native strings contain valid UTF-8 text.

You can store the imported function and call it with one or two arguments, or pass it directly to a callback whose string parameters are borrowed. Stored values adapt to one- or two-parameter callbacks with borrowed string parameters. Conditional calls selecting this same native operation through imports or stored values accept either argument count and evaluate the predicate once. A factory can also return the imported or stored function using a matching callable return annotation, such as (path: ReadOnly<string>) => string. A reusable borrowed callback contract borrows the stored value for the call. An owned callback contract or declaration move transfers it. An owned once contract permits one invocation, including when that callback is returned from a function; a second invocation is a compile-time error. An explicit closure can also select the argument list:

```tsrust
import { basename } from "std:path";
function main() {
    const nameOf = (path: string): string => { return basename(path); };
    console.log(nameOf("/tmp/report.txt")); // report.txt
}
```

Ownership: arguments are borrowed; the result owns its contents.

