std:path.documentation:
Returns the lexical parent of a path without reading the filesystem or resolving dot components. Accepts exactly one string path. Missing, extra and non-string arguments are compile-time errors. Returns independently owned text and leaves the input unchanged.

```tsrust
import { dirname } from "std:path";
function main() {
    console.log(dirname("/tmp/report.txt"));
} // output: /tmp
```

Empty input or a relative filename returns dot. Trailing separators are ignored. Original parent separators remain: dirname("a//b") returns "a/". POSIX dirname("//a") returns "//". Windows drive-relative names retain the drive prefix and UNC share roots remain intact. POSIX paths recognize slash; Windows paths recognize slash and backslash and drive-letter prefixes. Native strings contain valid UTF-8 text, preserved without Unicode normalization.

Ownership: path is borrowed; the result owns its contents.

examples.source:
import { dirname } from "std:path";
function main() {
    console.log(dirname("/tmp/report.txt"));
}

std:path.documentation:
Returns the suffix beginning at the final dot in the last path component without reading the filesystem. Accepts exactly one string path. Missing, extra and non-string arguments are compile-time errors. Returns independently owned text and leaves the input unchanged.

```tsrust
import { extname } from "std:path";
function main() {
    console.log(extname("report.tar.gz"));
} // output: .gz
```

Trailing separators are ignored. Empty input, a leading-dot filename, dot and dot-dot return empty text. A final dot returns dot; extname("...") returns dot. Only the final suffix is returned. POSIX paths recognize slash; Windows paths recognize slash and backslash and drive-letter prefixes. Native strings contain valid UTF-8 text, preserved without Unicode normalization.

Ownership: path is borrowed; the result owns its contents.

examples.source:
import { extname } from "std:path";
function main() {
    console.log(extname("report.tar.gz"));
}

std:path.documentation:
Tests whether a path starts with platform root syntax without reading the filesystem. Accepts exactly one string path. Missing, extra and non-string arguments are compile-time errors. Returns a boolean and leaves the input unchanged.

```tsrust
import { isAbsolute } from "std:path";
function main() {
    console.log(isAbsolute("/tmp/report.txt"));
} // output: true
```

Empty input is false. POSIX requires a leading slash. Windows accepts a leading slash or backslash, UNC syntax, and a drive letter followed by colon and a separator. C:report.txt is drive-relative and returns false. Dot components do not change root syntax. This test does not prove filesystem containment. Native strings contain valid UTF-8 text. Unicode content is preserved without Unicode normalization.

Ownership: path is borrowed; the result is a boolean value.

Returns a lexical path with repeated separators collapsed and dot components resolved without reading the filesystem. Accepts exactly one string path. Missing, extra and non-string arguments are compile-time errors. Returns independently owned text and leaves the input unchanged.

```tsrust
