    console.log(normalize("reports/../archive/"));
} // output: archive/
```

Empty input returns dot. Absolute paths cannot traverse above their root; leading relative parent components remain. A trailing separator remains. POSIX uses slash and collapses leading repeated slashes. Windows emits backslash, retains drive prefixes and UNC share roots, and protects relative colon paths from becoming drive paths. Device and reserved-name paths follow lexical spelling rules; repeated normalization can change special device forms, for example `CON:` normalizes to `.\CON:.` and then `CON:.`. No symlink resolution occurs. Normalization is not a containment check. Native strings contain valid UTF-8 text. Unicode content is preserved without Unicode normalization.

Ownership: path is borrowed; the result owns its contents.

Returns the lexical path from one location to another. Accepts exactly two strings, from and to; neither has a default. Missing, extra and non-string arguments are compile-time errors. Empty inputs use the current working directory. Relative inputs resolve against that directory before dot components and repeated separators are removed. Returns independently owned text and leaves both inputs unchanged.

```tsrust
import { relative } from "std:path";
