Object.documentation:
Returns the field values of a record as a new array, in the same order as `Object.keys`.

@param value - One record, tuple or array. A record gives its field values in the order of that value's constructor literal. A tuple gives its positions in order. An array gives a copy of itself.

@returns A new array. When every field has the same type the result is an ordinary array such as `int[]`. When field types differ the element type is the union of the field types, such as `(int | string)[]`; narrow an element with `typeof` or a null check before using it as one type.

```tsrust
type ValuesSize = { w: int; h: int; };
const size: ValuesSize = { h: 5, w: 4 };
const sides = Object.values(size);
console.log(sides, sides[0] + sides[1]); // prints "[ 5, 4 ] 9"
```

```tsrust
type ValuesRow = { id: int; name: string; };
const row: ValuesRow = { id: 7, name: "ada" };
for (const value of Object.values(row)) {
    if (typeof value === "string") { console.log(value.toUpperCase()); } else { console.log(value + 1); }
} // prints "8" then "ADA"
```

The value is evaluated once. Exactly one argument is required (`arity`). Strings, numbers, booleans, null, undefined, nullable values and records that have a function field are `unsupported`. A record must have at least one field.

Ownership: the argument is only read. Each value is copied, so the result is independent of the record; changing one does not change the other. JavaScript shares nested arrays and objects with the source instead.

