```tsrust
type EntriesRow = { id: int; name: string; };
const row: EntriesRow = { name: "ada", id: 7 };
for (const pair of Object.entries(row)) { console.log(pair[0], String(pair[1])); } // prints "name ada" then "id 7"
```

```tsrust
type EntriesSize = { w: int; h: int; };
const size: EntriesSize = { w: 4, h: 5 };
console.log(Object.entries(size)); // prints "[ [ 'w', 4 ], [ 'h', 5 ] ]"
```

The value is evaluated once. An array argument is `unsupported`; use `array.entries()` for `[index, value]` pairs with int positions. Exactly one argument is required (`arity`). Strings, numbers, booleans, null, undefined, nullable values and records that have a function field are `unsupported`. A record must have at least one field.

Ownership: the argument is only read. Each value is copied, so the result is independent of the record. JavaScript shares nested arrays and objects with the source instead.

Checks whether a value has its own property with the selected name. Inherited names such as toString do not count. A declared record field counts even when its value is undefined; a field named hasOwnProperty does not interfere.

@param value - Record, tuple, dense array, string, number or boolean to inspect. Nullable values, receiver unions and functions are unsupported.
@param key - Property name. Primitive keys are converted to text; strings are used directly. Object, array and callable keys are unsupported. Both arguments are required and evaluated once, value then key. Wrong argument count is an arity error.

Returns boolean without changing either argument. Arrays and tuples own length and each existing canonical nonnegative integer position. Strings own length and positions indexed by UTF-16 code units: é😀 owns 0, 1 and 2. Names such as 01 and -0 are not positions; the numeric key -0.0 becomes 0. Numbers and booleans have no supported own properties. Dynamic record names, sparse arrays and symbols are unsupported.

```tsrust
import { strictEqual } from "std:testing";
function main(){const values=[4,5];strictEqual(Object.hasOwn(values,"length"),true);strictEqual(Object.hasOwn(values,2),false);}
```

Ownership: the value and string key are borrowed; scalar keys are copied. No receiver data is cloned.

StringConstructor.documentation:
Builds a string from Unicode code points, one character per code, in argument order.

@param codes - Zero or more int code points from 0 through 1114111 (0x10FFFF). Spread an `int[]` to pass many: `String.fromCodePoint(...codes)`. A `number` or other type is a `type` error; convert first.

@returns A new string. No arguments, or an empty spread array, gives `""`.

```tsrust
const codes = [116, 115, 114];
console.log(String.fromCodePoint(65, 233, 128512), String.fromCodePoint(...codes)); // prints "Aé😀 tsr"
```

A code below 0 or above 1114111 stops the program with `String.fromCodePoint code point must be between 0 and 1114111` and the source position of the call. Every argument evaluates first, left to right.

Codes 55296 through 57343 (0xD800 to 0xDFFF) are surrogate halves, not characters. A high half followed by its low half forms one character. A half with no partner becomes U+FFFD (the replacement character), because a string always holds valid Unicode; JavaScript would keep the lone half.

The codes are only read; a spread array stays usable. The result is a new owned string.

Spread arguments accept arrays or Sets of int codes in iteration order; scalar code sources stay usable. Strings supply character values and are incompatible with int code parameters.

```tsrust
console.log(String.fromCodePoint(...new Set<int>([65,128578])));
// prints A🙂
```

Builds a string from UTF-16 code units, in argument order. Use it for codes up to 65535; use `String.fromCodePoint` for full Unicode code points.

@param codes - Zero or more int code units. Only the low 16 bits of each value are used, so 65601 gives the same character as 65. Spread an `int[]` to pass many: `String.fromCharCode(...codes)`. A `number` or other type is a `type` error; convert first.

@returns A new string. No arguments, or an empty spread array, gives `""`.

```tsrust
const codes = [72, 105];
console.log(String.fromCharCode(...codes), String.fromCharCode(55357, 56832)); // prints "Hi 😀"
```

A high surrogate (55296 to 56319) followed by a low surrogate (56320 to 57343) forms one character, as in the example. A surrogate with no partner becomes U+FFFD (the replacement character), because a string always holds valid Unicode; JavaScript would keep the lone unit. No value causes an error.

The codes are only read; a spread array stays usable. The result is a new owned string.

Spread arguments accept arrays or Sets of int units in iteration order; scalar code sources stay usable. Strings supply character values and are incompatible with int code parameters.

```tsrust
console.log(String.fromCharCode(...new Set<int>([65,66])));
// prints AB
```

Joins raw text segments with substitutions between them. Backslashes already present in the segments stay unchanged; escape sequences in source string literals are still decoded normally.

@param template - A record with a `raw: string[]` field. Its elements are text segments in order. An empty array returns `""`; one segment returns that segment. Declare the type for an empty array, for example `const empty: string[] = [];`.
@param substitutions - Optional values inserted between consecutive segments, converted with `String(value)`. Supported scalar values, arrays and records are accepted. Spread a `string[]` to insert several strings. There are no defaults other than empty text for missing substitutions. Extra substitutions are ignored in the result, but their expressions still evaluate.

Returns a new string. All argument expressions evaluate once, left to right. The template and substitution sources do not change.

```tsrust
type Template = { raw: string[]; };
const template: Template = { raw: ["Hello ", "!"] };
console.log(String.raw(template, "Ada")); // prints Hello Ada!
```

```tsrust
type Template = { raw: string[]; };
const template: Template = { raw: ["a", "b", "c"] };
const values = ["X", "Y"];
console.log(String.raw(template, ...values), String.raw(template, 7));
// prints aXbYc a7bc
```

No template is an `arity` error. A template without a `raw: string[]` field, or a non-string spread array, is rejected. Function-source conversions, arbitrary array-like objects, a string as the raw field and tagged-template syntax are unsupported. Segments hold valid Unicode text; lone surrogate halves are unavailable. Scalar conversions follow `String()`, including `null`, `undefined`, `NaN`, `Infinity` and zero for negative zero.

Ownership: the template is borrowed; substitutions are read and a spread string array is copied. All sources remain usable. The result is owned by the caller.

Spread substitutions accept string characters, string arrays and Sets of strings in insertion order. Sources stay usable; the result remains an independent owned string. Map tuple substitutions are incompatible with string spread elements.

```tsrust
const inserts=new Set<string>(["x","y"]);console.log(String.raw({raw:["<",":",">"]},...inserts),inserts.size);
// prints <x:y> 2
```

