MapConstructor.documentation:
Groups source elements by a callback key and returns a new Map whose values are independently owned arrays. Accepts a dense array, string, Set or Map. String elements are Unicode scalar character strings; Set elements are keys; Map elements are key/value tuples. Empty input returns an empty typed Map and invokes no callback.

@param source - Required ordered input. Evaluates once before the callback expression. Source values remain usable.
@param callback - Required reusable function supplied a readonly element and its zero-based int position. It may declare fewer parameters, defaults or a compatible rest array. Returns one supported Map key type: scalar, enum or compatible union. Number keys use SameValueZero, so NaN keys group together and both zeros share one group. Record, array and function identity keys are unsupported.

Groups appear in first-key order; elements inside each group retain input order. The callback can change unrelated captured state but cannot mutate or consume the source or its owning elements. Callback failures stop traversal and propagate through catch/finally. Wrong argument counts, incompatible callbacks, mutable element access and noncloneable source values are static errors. Arbitrary iterator objects and spread arguments are unsupported.

```tsrust
const groups=Map.groupBy([4,1,6,3],(value:int):int=>{return value%2;}); console.log(groups.size); const even=groups.get(0); if(even!==undefined){console.log(even.join(","));}
// prints 2 then 4,6
```

Native groups copy owning values independently; JavaScript groups retain object references. String positions count Unicode scalar characters, while string length and indexing retain UTF-8 byte semantics.

Ownership: grouping explicitly copies source values into independent output arrays. Ordinary access and bindings do not clone. A callback returning an owning borrowed value must use clone() or construct a fresh key.

std:bytes.documentation:
An owned contiguous byte buffer. Each element is a byte in 0..255; byte counts differ from Unicode character counts. This is a type declaration, so it takes no arguments and returns no value. Its storage fields are private; record literals cannot construct it. Use fromArray or alloc to construct a buffer, length to observe its byte count, at/set for checked access, and append/resize for growth. copy selects a checked independent byte range; clone(bytes) independently copies the whole buffer. Console inspection, string conversion, property reflection, object spread and value comparison are unsupported and report source diagnostics.

```tsrust
import { Bytes } from "std:bytes";
function accept(value: ReadOnly<Bytes>): void {}
function main() {}
```

This declares a borrowing parameter without creating or mutating a buffer. Ownership: passing by value transfers the buffer; ReadOnly borrows it without copying. Explicit clone creates independent storage.

