std:bytes.documentation:
Describes a byte operation failure. The code identifies the failure category, message explains it, and optional offset locates the offending byte or input element. An omitted offset reads as undefined. This is a record type, so the declaration takes no arguments and returns no value. Creating or reading an error does not change a byte buffer.

```tsrust
import { ByteError } from "std:bytes";
function main() {
    const error: ByteError = { code: "range", message: "invalid byte", offset: 2 };
    console.log(error.code, error.offset); // range 2
}
```

An offset counts bytes or array elements, never Unicode characters; its meaning depends on the failing operation. Ownership: the error owns its string fields; ReadOnly<ByteError> borrows the error.

code.documentation:
Identifies the failure category as a string. This property takes no arguments. Reading it leaves the error and byte buffers unchanged.

```tsrust
import { ByteError } from "std:bytes";
function main() {
    const error: ByteError = { code: "range", message: "invalid byte" };
    console.log(error.code); // range
}
```

Ownership: Borrow the string for inspection or clone explicitly to retain an independent value.

message.documentation:
Explains the failure as a string intended for people. This property takes no arguments. Reading it leaves the error and byte buffers unchanged.

```tsrust
import { ByteError } from "std:bytes";
function main() {
    const error: ByteError = { code: "range", message: "invalid byte" };
    console.log(error.message); // invalid byte
}
```

Ownership: Borrow the string for inspection or clone explicitly to retain an independent value.

offset.documentation:
Locates the offending byte or input element when supplied by an operation. An index-range error reports the requested index, which may be negative. This property takes no arguments and returns int | undefined. An omitted offset reads as undefined. Reading it leaves the error and byte buffers unchanged. An offset counts bytes or array elements, never Unicode characters.

```tsrust
import { ByteError } from "std:bytes";
function main() {
    const error: ByteError = { code: "range", message: "invalid byte" };
    console.log(error.offset); // undefined
}
```

Ownership: The scalar result copies; the error remains reusable.

std:bytes.documentation:
A readonly window onto a range of bytes that another value owns. It holds no bytes of its own: view creates it without copying, and it reads the owner's current bytes. This is a type declaration, so it takes no arguments and returns no value. It cannot be built with a record literal and it cannot be cloned; use copy to make independent Bytes. length, at, copy and toUtf8 read it, and view makes a narrower window of it. While a ByteView is in scope, its owner can be read, and it cannot be written, resized, moved or replaced. A ByteView can be stored in records, arrays and callbacks, passed to functions and returned from a function whose parameter owns the bytes. It cannot outlive its owner.

```tsrust
import { ByteView, fromArray, view, length } from "std:bytes";
function size(part: ReadOnly<ByteView>): int { return length(part); }
function main() { match (fromArray([1, 2, 3])) { Ok(bytes) => { match (view(bytes, 1, 3)) { Ok(part) => { console.log(size(part)); }, Err(error) => { console.log(error.message); }, } }, Err(error) => { console.log(error.message); }, } }
```

Expected output: 2.

Ownership: borrows the owner's bytes for as long as the view is in scope. Passing it as ReadOnly<ByteView> lends the window; passing it by value moves the window, never the bytes.

