std:bytes.documentation:
An exclusive, writable window onto a range of bytes that another value owns. viewMut creates it without copying, and set writes through it into the owner. This is a type declaration, so it takes no arguments and returns no value. It cannot be built with a record literal or cloned. length, at, copy and toUtf8 read it, set writes it, and view or viewMut make a narrower window of it. While a ByteViewMut is in scope, its owner cannot be read, written, resized, moved or replaced, and no other view of the same bytes can exist. The window has a fixed length: it cannot grow or shrink the owner.

```tsrust
import { ByteViewMut, fromArray, viewMut, set, at } from "std:bytes";
function clear(part: Mutable<ByteViewMut>) { match (set(part, 0, 0)) { Ok(_) => {}, Err(error) => { console.log(error.message); }, } }
function main() { match (fromArray([7, 8])) { Ok(bytes) => { match (viewMut(bytes, 1, 2)) { Ok(part) => { clear(part); }, Err(error) => { console.log(error.message); }, } match (at(bytes, 1)) { Ok(value) => { console.log(value); }, Err(error) => { console.log(error.message); }, } }, Err(error) => { console.log(error.message); }, } }
```

Expected output: 0.

Ownership: borrows the owner's bytes exclusively for as long as the view is in scope. Passing it as Mutable<ByteViewMut> lends the window for writing. Writes are visible in the owner after the view's scope ends.

